{
 "coreName": "Cemu",
 "systemId": "WIIU",
 "systemNames": {
  "WIIU": "Wii U"
 },
 "author": "The Cemu project and its contributors; chimera port by Sergio Martin",
 "url": "https://github.com/ToolAssisted-run/chimera-core-cemu",
 "runsOn": [
  "windows-x86_64",
  "linux-x86_64"
 ],
 "romFile": "game",
 "deterministic": true,
 "memoryLayoutMiB": [
  1024,
  16,
  64,
  64,
  12288
 ],
 "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. The console's 4 GiB address space is one reservation in the mmap heap, beside the recompiler's table and the emulator's own heap; only the pages a game touches cost anything. Keep in step with waterbox/run-wbx.c.",
 "video": {
  "_comment": "The buffer's capacity: the TV's 1280x720 above the GamePad's 854x480, centred under it. The Screens setting shows both (the default), the TV alone or the GamePad alone, so the live size comes from GetVideoWidth and GetVideoHeight. Cemu's OpenGL renderer draws through the GPU bridge and the picture is read back when a frame ends. The vertical blank comes every 1001/60000 s of the machine's time, counted from when it was switched on, as the console's does.",
  "width": 1280,
  "height": 1200,
  "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
  "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
  "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra",
  "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
  "_comment_gpuStates": "A load is a new graphics context. The renderer is made again on it (its caches emptied and filled again from the console's memory) before the GPU's next command. The tests' proof: a new context before every frame, and a state carried into another process, end with the pictures of a run that was never stopped (waterbox/run-gate.sh, picture:remade and states:session).",
  "greenzoneSurvivesReload": true,
  "_comment_greenzone": "TRUE (owner, 2026-11-04): a project's greenzone this core drew on a GPU may be loaded by a later session (docs/gpu-bridge.md in Chimera, 'Keeping the greenzone across sessions'). Measured 2026-11-04 with New Super Mario Bros. U through chimera-run, on llvmpipe: a 1.99 GB greenzone kept over 11,900 frames, into the game's first level, by one process and loaded by another, which sought to frame 11,750 at once (29 s, where playing there takes 14 minutes): the straight run's MEM2 and picture, identical; a control frame (11,600) another picture; no guest death. The same history sought into on Windows (GTX 1060): Linux's MEM2. A history kept by a Windows process over the same frames, sought into by another Windows process (16 s) and by a Linux one (39 s): Linux's straight MEM2 at 11,750 both times, and on Linux its picture too. Chimera's window (Linux, headless) played the movie to 11,900, saved and quit; a new window opened the project and sought to 11,750 in 67 s (playing there took 21 minutes): MEM2 the straight run's, by its SHA-256. The test programs too, on Linux and Windows (run-frontend.sh engine:history, run-windows.sh windows:history). docs/PLAN.md has the rest.",
  "_comment_renderer": "The renderer setting has one value, opengl-hw: Cemu's OpenGL renderer on the host's graphics card through the GPU bridge. Its value ends in -hw because that is how Chimera's window knows to give a core the bridge (docs/gpu-bridge.md in Chimera, 'Turning it on'); chimera-run gives it with --gpu."
 },
 "audio": {
  "_comment": "What the program hands the console's TV output: 48 kHz stereo, in blocks of 576 pairs (12 ms), about 801 pairs a frame. The GamePad's speaker is not heard.",
  "rate": 48000,
  "samplesPerFrame": 4096,
  "channels": 2,
  "get": "GetAudio"
 },
 "lag": {
  "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
 },
 "input": {
  "name": "Wii U GamePad",
  "_comment": "The GamePad: its buttons, its two sticks (-32768 to 32767, up and right positive) and its touch screen (Touch held, at the point Touch X and Touch Y name: 0 to 65535, a fraction of the whole picture; it touches only where the picture shows the GamePad's screen). Its motion sensors answer that it is held still, face up.",
  "buttons": [
   "P1 Up",
   "P1 Down",
   "P1 Left",
   "P1 Right",
   "P1 A",
   "P1 B",
   "P1 X",
   "P1 Y",
   "P1 L",
   "P1 R",
   "P1 ZL",
   "P1 ZR",
   "P1 Plus",
   "P1 Minus",
   "P1 Home",
   "P1 Left Stick",
   "P1 Right Stick",
   "P1 Touch"
  ],
